# FORMSENTRY — FUTURISTIC 3D UI INTEGRATION PROMPT

Act as a senior Three.js developer, creative frontend engineer, and interactive UI designer.

Upgrade my existing FormSentry DPDP Form Auditor into a futuristic, visually striking **Privacy Investigation Lab**. Recreate the visual concepts of an animated 3D scanning grid and interactive data particles using original code.

Important: React Bits is inaccessible in my environment. Do not attempt to open it, install it, or copy its source code. Build equivalent visual effects independently.

## 1. Inspect before modifying

Inspect the existing project and identify:
- The current framework and folder structure.
- The existing entry point.
- The working form analysis engine.
- The Privacy Detective and Privacy Surgery features.
- Existing styles, event handlers, and dependencies.

Preserve the current application and make focused changes. Do not rewrite working features or replace the existing project with a new template.

If the application uses plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, keep that architecture. Do not migrate to React, Next.js, TypeScript, or a build system just to add 3D effects.

## 2. Visual identity

Product: FormSentry  
Tagline: Collect less. Explain more.

Create a premium dark interface inspired by futuristic investigation rooms and advanced privacy analysis systems.

Palette:
- Near-black graphite background.
- Electric lime green accents.
- White and cool-grey text.
- Amber for fields requiring review.
- Restrained coral red for likely excessive collection.

Use modern typography, clean spacing, subtle borders, and deliberate visual hierarchy.

Avoid generic AI-dashboard styling, excessive neon, purple gradients, heavy glassmorphism, constant motion, and unnecessary decorative elements.

## 3. Build an original 3D scanning grid

Use Three.js to create a perspective grid environment.

Requirements:
- Dark floor grid extending into the distance.
- Thin lime-green grid lines.
- A restrained scanning beam travelling across the grid.
- Subtle depth and perspective.
- A few small floating data particles.
- Slow, smooth movement.
- Optional subtle mouse-based camera parallax.

The grid should resemble a privacy investigation scanner, not a racing game or sci-fi shooter.

Create the scene using native Three.js geometry and materials. Do not require external 3D models, textures, or inaccessible component libraries.

Place this scene behind the hero section, not behind every page element.

The heading, input editor, purpose selector, and Analyze button must remain readable.

## 4. Create an interactive data-particle visualization

Build a lightweight particle visualization using Three.js.

Represent analyzed form fields as points in a contained 3D space.

Use the actual analysis results:
- Fields likely necessary: lime markers.
- Fields potentially necessary or requiring justification: amber markers.
- Fields likely excessive: restrained coral markers.
- Unknown fields: neutral markers.

If particle-to-field mapping is implemented, clicking a particle should select the corresponding field and open its actual evidence panel.

If that interaction would destabilize the application, use an aggregate visualization driven by real counts and retain the existing HTML findings table for detailed inspection.

When the user switches between the original and proposed minimized form, update the visualization using the actual field lists.

Do not create random particles that falsely imply real findings.

## 5. Connect the visuals to the real analysis engine

The visual effects must reflect application state.

Before analysis:
- Display the calm idle scene.

When Analyze is clicked:
- Start the scanning animation.
- Execute the actual rule-based analysis.
- Display results after the real analysis completes.
- Stop the scanning animation.

When the purpose changes:
- Recalculate field necessity.
- Update the findings and visualization.
- Clear stale results when appropriate.

When analysis fails:
- Display a useful error.
- Return the scene to its idle state.

Never use fake progress percentages, fabricated findings, or artificial delays merely to make the interface look impressive.

The Three.js scene must not perform or replace the privacy analysis.

## 6. Privacy Surgery transformation

Make the before-and-after comparison visually memorable.

Show:
- Original form fields.
- Proposed minimized form fields.
- Fields recommended for removal.
- Fields recommended to be optional or reviewed.
- Fields recommended to remain.

When the user changes the proposed form:
- Update the real field lists and counts.
- Animate the corresponding particles or visual markers.
- Preserve the original audit findings.
- Allow removed fields to be restored.
- Keep user overrides separate from engine recommendations.

The animation should reinforce the actual transformation, not pretend that a privacy improvement occurred when no underlying data changed.

## 7. Performance and compatibility

- Use a compatible, pinned Three.js version.
- Avoid heavy post-processing and large shader systems.
- Limit particle counts and rendering complexity.
- Cap device pixel ratio.
- Resize the renderer correctly.
- Pause or reduce animation when the browser tab is hidden.
- Respect the prefers-reduced-motion setting.
- Provide a static CSS fallback if WebGL fails.
- Reduce or disable 3D on low-performance mobile devices.
- Avoid duplicate animation loops and memory leaks.
- Clean up event listeners and rendering resources.
- Keep all form controls clickable and accessible.

If the current project is opened directly through file://, verify whether the chosen module-loading approach works. If a local server is required, document that honestly and provide the simplest reliable launch method.

Keep the core application functional if Three.js fails to load.

## 8. Privacy requirements

All form analysis must remain local to the browser.

Do not:
- Upload HTML or field names.
- Send analysis to an external AI service.
- Log user-provided form content.
- Persist pasted input unnecessarily.
- Execute scripts contained in pasted HTML.

The 3D effects must not transmit user data to any external service.

